From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] shader_recompiler: give every shader its own clip distances

`spirv_emit_context.h` declared `static std::bitset<8> clip_distance_written;` at namespace scope in
a header. That is one object for the whole process, shared by every shader translation and never
reset. EmitSetAttribute set a bit when a shader wrote a clip distance, and EmitPrologue skipped
initialising exactly the distances whose bit was set.

So the prologue's behaviour depended on which shaders had been translated before it. Once any shader
in the session wrote clip distance 0, every later shader that declares clip distances left distance 0
uninitialised, and an uninitialised clip distance can clip whole primitives away - objects that
vanish and stay vanished for the rest of the session, which is how Fritchii lists this fix ("objects
that could vanish for a whole session"). It is also a data race: the bitset is written from whatever
threads translate shaders, with no synchronisation, so concurrent translations could see and act on
each other's bits.

The fix is to stop tracking it. The prologue initialises every user clip distance unconditionally,
which is what it did before the bitset existed, and the two writes to the bitset go with it.

What this can break: a shader that writes a clip distance now has that distance initialised to zero
and then overwritten, instead of being left alone. That is one extra store per clip distance per
prologue, and it is what SPIR-V requires anyway - a variable read before it is written is undefined.
The only cost is a handful of instructions in vertex and geometry prologues.

Ported from Frizti/Fritchii ff0f6aa30, which is zortos293/Lichii #902.

Measured or a guess: the mechanism is certain - a process-wide, never-reset static in a header, read
by the prologue of every shader that declares clip distances - and upstream connects it to objects
vanishing for a session. Whether any object in our own screenshots vanished this way is not
established; nothing in our log names it. It is included because it is a real correctness and
thread-safety defect with a trivial fix.

diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
index 01c22741d3..f151b94cc6 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_context_get_set.cpp
@@ -441,13 +441,6 @@ void EmitSetAttribute(EmitContext& ctx, IR::Attribute attr, Id value, [[maybe_un
         value = ctx.OpBitcast(output->type, value);
     }
 
-    static constexpr IR::Attribute cd0 = IR::Attribute::ClipDistance0;
-    static constexpr IR::Attribute cd7 = IR::Attribute::ClipDistance7;
-
-    if (attr >= cd0 && attr <= cd7) {
-        const u32 idx = (u32) attr - (u32) cd0;
-        clip_distance_written.set(idx);
-    }
     ctx.OpStore(output->pointer, value);
 }
 
diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_special.cpp b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_special.cpp
index d621cdc116..cb77b524aa 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_special.cpp
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/emit_spirv_special.cpp
@@ -124,13 +124,18 @@ void EmitPrologue(EmitContext& ctx) {
             }
         }
 
+        // Every clip distance is initialised, unconditionally. This used to skip any distance that a
+        // previous translation had written, through a `static std::bitset<8>` in the header that no
+        // shader ever reset: once one shader wrote clip distance 0, every later shader left its
+        // distance 0 uninitialised, and an uninitialised distance can clip whole objects away for
+        // the rest of the session (zortos293/Lichii #902; Fritchii lists it as "objects that could
+        // vanish for a whole session"). That bitset was also a single shared object, written
+        // without synchronisation, across the threads that translate shaders.
         if (Sirit::ValidId(ctx.clip_distances)) {
             for (u32 i = 0; i < ctx.profile.max_user_clip_distances; ++i) {
-                if (!clip_distance_written.test(i)) {
-                    const Id idx = ctx.Const(i);
-                    const Id element = ctx.OpAccessChain(ctx.output_f32, ctx.clip_distances, idx);
-                    ctx.OpStore(element, ctx.Const(0.0f));
-                }
+                const Id idx = ctx.Const(i);
+                const Id element = ctx.OpAccessChain(ctx.output_f32, ctx.clip_distances, idx);
+                ctx.OpStore(element, ctx.Const(0.0f));
             }
         }
     }
diff --git a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.h b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.h
index 5c4595ceab..0c76ebc9fc 100644
--- a/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.h
+++ b/src/shader_recompiler/backend/spirv/spirv_emit_context.h
@@ -31,8 +31,6 @@ namespace Shader::Backend::SPIRV {
 /// depth-compare typing was already here; the AMD-Windows compiler stall list is not for RADV.
 [[nodiscard]] bool AmdPr273Fixes();
 
-static std::bitset<8> clip_distance_written;
-
 using Sirit::Id;
 
 class VectorTypes {
